@node Backend Overview
@section Backend Overview

EIGHTBOL supports compilation to 15 different processor architectures spanning
8-bit to 32-bit systems. Each backend translates the EIGHTBOL Abstract Syntax
Tree (AST) to native assembly language optimized for the target processor.

The 15 supported backends are:

@table @asis
@item 6502 (@code{backend-6502})
MOS Technology 6502 8-bit processor (Commodore 64, Apple II, NES, etc.)

@item 65c02 (@code{backend-65c02})
WDC 65c02 8-bit processor (enhanced 6502 with additional instructions)

@item 65c816 (@code{backend-65c816})
WDC 65c816 16-bit processor (extended addressing, banking support)

@item ARM7 (@code{backend-arm7})
32-bit ARM7 (Thumb) processor for Game Boy Advance and embedded systems

@item CP1610 (@code{backend-cp1610})
General Instrument CP1610 16-bit processor (Mattel Intellivision)

@item F8 (@code{backend-f8})
Fairchild 3850 CPU 8-bit processor (Fairchild Channel F console)

@item FORTH (@code{backend-forth})
Stack-based FORTH virtual machine for portable code execution

@item HuC6280 (@code{backend-huc6280})
Hudson Soft HuC6280 8-bit processor (PC Engine/TurboGrafx-16)

@item i286 (@code{backend-i286})
Intel 80286 16-bit processor (protected mode x86, DOS, OS/2)

@item m6800 (@code{backend-m6800})
Motorola 6800 8-bit processor (predecessor to 68000 family)

@item m68k (@code{backend-m68k})
Motorola 68000 16/32-bit processor (Amiga, Atari ST, Mac, etc.)

@item RP2A03 (@code{backend-rp2a03})
Ricoh 2A03 processor variant of 6502 (Nintendo Entertainment System/NES)

@item SM83 (@code{backend-sm83})
Sharp SM83 Z80-derived processor (Nintendo Game Boy)

@item Stack (@code{backend-stack})
Abstract stack machine for virtual execution and portability

@item Z80 (@code{backend-z80})
Zilog Z80 8-bit processor (ZX Spectrum, MSX, TRS-80, Game Boy, etc.)
@end table

@node Processor Family Matrix
@section Processor Family Matrix

Processors can be grouped by architecture family and bit width:

@subsection 6502 Family (8-bit Stack-Based)

@table @asis
@item 6502
Original MOS Technology design, single accumulator architecture

@item 65c02
Enhanced version with additional instructions (BRA, TSB, TRB, etc.)

@item 65c816
Extended addressing modes and 16-bit capabilities (Processor Direct Page, Bank Registers)

@item RP2A03
Custom variant for NES console (6502 core with modified audio)

@item HuC6280
Hudson custom variant (PC Engine/TurboGrafx-16)

@item SM83
Sharp's Z80-derived for Game Boy (hybrid design)
@end table

@subsection Z80 Family (8-bit Register-Based)

@table @asis
@item Z80
Zilog's enhanced 8080 design with extended instruction set

@item SM83
Game Boy processor (Z80 core with modifications)
@end table

@subsection 68000 Family (16/32-bit Register-Based)

@table @asis
@item m6800
Motorola's first-generation 8-bit processor

@item m68k
Motorola's 16/32-bit processor family (MC68000, MC68010, etc.)
@end table

@subsection Intel x86 Family (16/32-bit Register-Based)

@table @asis
@item i286
Intel's 16-bit processor (real and protected modes)
@end table

@subsection Game Console Processors

@table @asis
@item 6502 (NES via RP2A03)
@item RP2A03 (custom 6502 variant)
@item SM83 (Game Boy)
@item HuC6280 (PC Engine/TurboGrafx-16)
@item CP1610 (Intellivision)
@item ARM7 (Game Boy Advance)
@end table

