@node Code Generation Strategy by Backend
@section Code Generation Strategy by Backend

@subsection Stack-Based Backends (6502 Family, Z80)

Stack-based backends use the hardware stack for:
@itemize
@item Function call returns (automatic via JSR/CALL)
@item Local variable storage
@item Return value passing
@end itemize

@subsection Register-Based Backends (68000, ARM7, i286)

Register-based backends prioritize register allocation:
@itemize
@item Minimize register spills to memory
@item Use multiple registers for value passing
@item Cache frequently-used variables in registers
@end itemize

@subsection Virtual Machine Backends (FORTH, Stack)

Virtual machine backends generate bytecode:
@itemize
@item Processor-independent intermediate code
@itemize
@item Executed by runtime interpreter
@itemize
@item Optimal for cross-platform deployment
@end itemize

@section Optimization Techniques by Backend

@subsection All Backends

@itemize
@item Dead code elimination
@item Constant propagation
@item Common subexpression elimination
@item Loop unrolling
@item Tail-call optimization
@end itemize

@subsection 6502 Family

@itemize
@item Zero-page optimization (fast memory area)
@item Addressing mode selection
@item Branch target optimization
@item Decimal mode usage (BCD arithmetic)
@end itemize

@subsection 68000 Family

@itemize
@item Address register optimization
@item Data register caching
@item Motorola addressing mode selection
@item Coprocessor instruction generation
@end itemize

@subsection ARM7

@itemize
@item Thumb code density optimization
@item Conditional execution elimination of branches
@item Register allocation per ARM calling convention
@item Literal pool management
@end itemize

@section Benchmarking and Performance

Different backends provide different performance characteristics:

@subsection Code Size

Most compact: ARM7 (Thumb), Z80
Most verbose: FORTH (VM overhead)

@subsection Execution Speed

Fastest: m68k, ARM7 (native execution, extended architecture)
Moderate: 6502, Z80, CP1610 (8-bit single-accumulator)
Slower: FORTH, Stack (interpretation overhead)

@subsection Register Pressure

Lowest: m68k (16 registers), ARM7 (16 registers)
Moderate: i286 (4 registers), CP1610 (multiple registers)
Highest: 6502 (1 accumulator), Z80 (1 accumulator)

@section Cross-Backend Compatibility

@subsection Language Feature Support

All backends support the full EIGHTBOL language feature set:
@itemize
@item Variable declarations and scope
@item Arithmetic and logical operations
@item Control flow (if/then/else, loops, procedures)
@item Subroutine calls with return values
@item Method invocation on objects
@item String manipulation and I/O
@item Memory access
@item Assembly integration
@end itemize

@subsection ABI Compatibility

Different ABIs (Application Binary Interfaces) are used:
@itemize
@item Stack-based: Parameter passing via stack
@itemize
@item Register-based: Parameter passing via registers
@itemize
@item Hybrid: Parameter passing via registers + stack
@end itemize

See individual backend chapters for specific ABI details.

@subsection Numeric Precision

All backends support:
@itemize
@item 8-bit integers
@itemize
@item 16-bit integers
@itemize
@item 32-bit integers (where hardware supports)
@itemize
@item Binary fixed-point arithmetic
@itemize
@item BCD/Decimal arithmetic (where supported)
@end itemize

Specific precision support varies by backend. See individual chapters for details.
